WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.245
Yann LeCun: language models will not reach human-level intelligence.

2
00:00:04.525 --> 00:00:15.768
LeCun is a Turing Award winner and was Meta’s chief AI scientist until he left to lead AMI Labs, a company pursuing a different approach, where he is executive chairman.

3
00:00:16.048 --> 00:00:18.544
His position has been consistent for years.

4
00:00:18.824 --> 00:00:39.027
In his 2022 position paper “A Path Towards Autonomous Machine Intelligence,” he argued that human-level intelligence requires world models: systems that predict how the world changes in response to actions, learned mostly from observation (video, sensor data) rather than text, and used for planning.

5
00:00:39.307 --> 00:00:45.941
He proposed a family of architectures (joint embedding predictive architectures) for learning such models.

6
00:00:46.221 --> 00:00:55.885
At Davos in January 2026, as reported by Fortune, he said that training on text alone would never get to human-level intelligence.

7
00:00:56.165 --> 00:01:14.171
In a lecture at Brown University on 1 April 2026, he called language models essentially a dead end for that goal, argued that current systems have no concept of how their actions affect the world, which makes most agentic systems risky, and said that at best, within five years,

8
00:01:14.451 --> 00:01:19.933
the field might be convinced it is on a good path toward human-level intelligence, but not there.

9
00:01:20.213 --> 00:01:34.869
A related argument you will hear in engineering discussions, often attached to critiques like his, is about compounding error: if each generated step has a small independent chance of going wrong, a long chain fails almost surely.

10
00:01:35.149 --> 00:01:39.608
The Predict below makes that concrete, and shows where the argument’s assumption breaks.
